ission to protect minors from inappropriate video game content."}, {"sourceSlug": "game-design", "sourceName": "Game Design", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "entertainment-software-rating-board", "targetName": "Entertainment Software Rating Board", "context": "The ESRB's rating system has created a new paradigm in game design, encouraging developers to consider age-appropriateness in their content."}], "places": [{"name": "New York City, USA", "role": "Headquarters"}, {"name": "Los Angeles, USA", "role": "Major Industry Hub"}, {"name": "San Francisco, USA", "role": "Gaming Development Center"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Entertainment Software Rating Board", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "entertainment-software-rating-board"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q191458", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Entertainment_Software_Rating_Board"], "tags": ["United States", "nonprofit organization", "Americas", "Humanitarian & Relief Organizations", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Entertainment Software Rating Board contributed to the historical record of the Contemporary period. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped institutional landscape and community development. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The ESRB has significantly influenced the video game industry's approach to content regulation, impacting over 40,000 games and shaping consumer expectations, thereby legitimizing video games as a cultural and educational medium.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-04T12:56:18Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28706783374", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "North american self-regulatory organization that assigns age and content ratings for video games Headquartered in New York City. Founded 1994.", "newValue": "The Entertainment Software Rating Board (ESRB), established in 1994, is a pivotal self-regulatory organization based in New York City, responsible for assigning age and content ratings to video games in North America. Its inception was a direct response to the growing concerns surrounding violent and explicit content in video games, particularly following the controversies surrounding titles like "Mortal Kombat" and "Night Trap". The formation of the ESRB marked a significant moment in the evolution of the video game industry, as it aimed to create a standardized system to inform consumers and protect minors from inappropriate material.

In its early years, the ESRB implemented a detailed rating system that includes categories such as "E for Everyone", "T for Teen", and "M for Mature". This framework not only provided clarity for consumers but also influenced game design and marketing strategies. By 2005, the ESRB had rated over 40,000 games, engaging with developers, retailers, and parents to promote responsible gaming. Its ratings have become a critical component of the video game industry's ecosystem, ensuring that players can make informed choices based on age-appropriateness and content suitability.

